// GitHub issue #445: the first thing in this kernel that actually excludes
// a second core.
//
// A test-and-set lock. Taking it is one acquire swap; giving it back is one
// release store. That pair is enough to build a lock because a swap is
// atomic: exactly one of two cores swapping into the same word can observe
// the free value, and the other observes what the winner wrote.
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-27). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - THE LOCK IS AN ADDRESS, and nothing checks that the address is a lock.
//   `usize` is what a slot address, a payload address and a lock word all
//   look like (GitHub issue #396). A typed `SpinLock` wrapper is the
//   obvious fix and is deliberately not here yet: it would need a cast to
//   reach the raw layer anyway, and there is exactly one caller today.
//   Add it with the second one.
// - NO OWNER, NO DEPTH, NO DEADLOCK DETECTION. Taking the same lock twice
//   on one core hangs, and this file cannot tell that from waiting for
//   another core. What checks lock ORDER is
//   `linux_user/intrusive_pool/pool_lock_check.tkb`, in the tier that can
//   afford to look; what would catch re-entrancy at runtime is an owner
//   field this has no caller for.
// - NOTHING HERE MASKS INTERRUPTS. On one core an interrupt handler that
//   takes a lock the interrupted code already holds deadlocks, and the
//   caller is what prevents that -- see `kernel/lib/pool_lock.tkb`, which
//   is this file's only caller and does the masking around it. Do not use
//   these three functions directly from code that can be interrupted.
// - THE WAIT IS A BUSY SPIN. No WFE, no backoff, no queue, so a contended
//   lock burns the waiting core. That is the right first shape at two
//   cores and the wrong one at many; issue #9 is where fairness and
//   affinity get decided, and there is no measurement to design against
//   until cores actually contend.

// Two values, not a bool: the word is what the atomic operates on, and an
// atomic works in usize.
const SPIN_LOCK_FREE: usize = 0;
const SPIN_LOCK_HELD: usize = 1;

// One attempt. True means this caller now holds it.
//
// Public rather than an internal step of spin_lock, and the reason is that
// it is the only way to test the exclusion single-threaded: take it, watch
// a second attempt fail, give it back, watch a third succeed. With one
// thread there is no other way to observe that the lock excludes anything,
// and "it compiles" is not evidence a lock works.
fn spin_trylock(lock: usize) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    let previous: usize = unsafe { atomic_swap_acquire(lock, SPIN_LOCK_HELD) };
    return previous == SPIN_LOCK_FREE;
}

// Blocks until taken.
//
// Test-and-TEST-and-set: the retry spins on a plain acquire LOAD and only
// swaps again once the word looks free. The obvious loop -- swapping over
// and over -- is correct but writes the cache line on every attempt, so
// each waiting core steals the line from the holder and from every other
// waiter. Reading instead lets the line stay shared until it actually
// changes. On one core this is indistinguishable; it is written this way
// because two cores are the point.
fn spin_lock(lock: usize) !{unsafe} {
    while (spin_trylock(lock) == false) {
        while (unsafe { atomic_load_acquire(lock) } != SPIN_LOCK_FREE) {
        }
    }
}

// Gives it back. A release store, so everything this core did while holding
// the lock is visible to whichever core takes it next -- that ordering, not
// the write itself, is the entire reason this is not `*lock = 0`.
fn spin_unlock(lock: usize) !{unsafe} {
    unsafe { atomic_store_release(lock, SPIN_LOCK_FREE); }
}

// Whether the word is currently held, for a probe or a diagnostic. Never a
// basis for deciding to enter a critical section: the answer can be stale
// before the caller acts on it, which is what spin_trylock is for.
fn spin_lock_is_held(lock: usize) -> bool !{unsafe} {
    return unsafe { atomic_load_acquire(lock) } != SPIN_LOCK_FREE;
}
